import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.06 Human Nature, Evolution, Habits, and the Capability to Act","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.06 Human Nature, Evolution, Habits, and the Capability to Act","xkChars":4,"xkWords":545,"xkLessons":{"D.06":{"title":"D.06 Human nature, evolution, habits, and capacity for action","lead":"Someone actually signing a document does not mean they can safely say no; a behavior remaining stable for a long time does not mean it is permanently written into genes. Explaining human action requires separating formation, circumstances, choices, and responsibility, then connecting them according to the evidence.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5082–5097; source anchor section-d-06; short quotation: ‘That constrained people actually act, can safely refuse, give valid consent, and bear a particular responsibility remain four different judgments’ (translated from Chinese). The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","Current function, historical adaptation, drift, cultural transmission, individual intention, and welfare consequences answer different questions. Behavior beneficial now may have originated under other conditions and acquired this function later. A retained cultural form may involve transmission, constraints, or path continuity rather than endorsement by every member. Welfare consequences concern who benefits or is harmed; individual intention concerns the actor's reasons. Good outcomes cannot establish good intentions retrospectively, nor does historical existence imply that something ought to be retained.","Cooperation may arise from kinship, reciprocity, reputation, partner choice, or institutional constraints. Analysis should specify who can observe behavior, who responds afterward, what benefits and costs are anticipated, and who bears the actual costs. Similar cooperative appearances cannot establish the same mechanism. Without responders or information channels, “reputation” alone cannot explain everything. Several mechanisms may coexist, but their respective material must be specified rather than switching stories arbitrarily to keep the conclusion invulnerable.","Personality differences may appear as conditionally stable tendencies, but slow change does not mean heredity, immutability, or essence. Repeated similar behavior may jointly arise from habit, environmental selection, opportunity, situational cues, and bodily skills. Cross-context records must also examine who entered which situations and whether measurements are comparable. Checking situations first is an investigative order, not a prior verdict that personality has no role. Comparisons of personality, situation, and interaction need proportionate information and evaluation conditions; rich situational material must not be compared with crude personality labels to declare a theoretical victory.","General learning may be supported by practice, performance, and formation processes, for example when skill changes have entered the current state. The historical increment (§4.5) asks the narrower question of what increment history still provides beyond a specified current representation. Skill learning having occurred can coexist with no observed extra historical increment; failing this narrow condition does not mean no learning occurred. Environmental opportunities and bodily practice also cannot be reduced to an abstract incentive score. Preserve what people can actually do, know, and bear.","Constrained actions may still be decisions made by actors, but that does not make them freely given consent. Separate actual behavior, information at the time, local control, options for refusal, consequences, and attribution of responsibility. The source’s employee signing a record under credible punishment is a teaching example: signing actually occurred, safe refusal may have been unavailable, and valid consent requires separate examination. Designers, supervisors, and beneficiaries may bear different responsibilities along the chains of rules, commands, and benefits. “They chose it themselves” must not erase the control chain; constraint also must not be equated with having no agency whatsoever.","High cost does not automatically establish sincerity, love, or identification. Costs may arise from coercion, misjudgment, lack of alternatives, reputational incentives, or other causes; actual situations and refutable material must be examined. Low-cost goodwill does not lose ordinary value because it fails the formal conditions of “Open-Ended Undertaking Action” (§7.3.11). Formal outcome records and ordinary help, care, and meaning are not one ranking of value. High cost must not become a universal credential for judging love or moral status.","The explanatory contributions of situation, personality, and their interaction may overlap. If all shared explanation is first assigned to situation and personality’s residual is then zero, this establishes at most no additional increment under that representation and ordering, not the nonexistence of personality-related variables. Assigning it first to personality and then denying situation is equally mistaken. Explain the distinctions among shared explanation, independent increments, and interaction; do not treat a statistical allocation rule as an ontological conclusion about people.","Behavioral differences caused by a treatment belong to the complete manipulated condition package; they must not be arbitrarily divided into an independent effect of one component. Hurry instructions may simultaneously change available time, expected responsibility, tension, and attention. Even if an experiment finds between-group differences, it does not automatically identify these mediators separately. Answering the effect of one component requires additional design and material. Nor may observed group differences be elevated into fixed personality judgments about any individual.","Neither natural selection nor social selection directly generates an ought. Successful persistence of a form may be an object of causal research, but does not thereby confer normative legitimacy. Institutional reward for a behavior also does not establish that it should be encouraged. Responsibility judgments likewise require explicit normative premises, control capacity, information, and specific consequences; evolutionary stories or the bare fact of a constrained person’s action cannot complete all attribution of responsibility.","The eight topic entries cover, in order, human nature and situationism; selection, adaptation, and drift; cultural evolution and dual inheritance; cooperation mechanisms; personality–situation interaction; freedom, responsibility, and capability; habit, embodiment, and environment; and explanations of action beyond incentives. Names such as Boyd and Richerson indicate research leads, not adoption of the whole of cultural-evolution research as an undifferentiated axiom of human nature. The strongest objection is that emphasizing situations excuses everyone. The response is to allocate responsibility more precisely across capability, information, control, and design chains, rather than replace one extreme with another."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Currently beneficial function","leftText":"A behavior presently has some beneficial consequence.","rightTitle":"Historically arose because of that function","rightText":"The origin mechanism needs separate historical and comparative material."},{"leftTitle":"Conditionally stable tendency","leftText":"A behavior pattern recurring under certain conditions.","rightTitle":"An immutable hereditary essence","rightText":"Slow change does not establish this; environment and practice still need consideration."},{"leftTitle":"An action actually occurred","leftText":"Someone performed an action and may have had local control.","rightTitle":"Safe refusal and valid consent","rightText":"Alternatives, pressure, information, and authorization conditions also need examination."},{"leftTitle":"Condition-package effect","leftText":"The difference produced by the complete manipulated arrangement.","rightTitle":"An independent effect of one component","rightText":"Needs an additional discriminating design; attribution cannot be arbitrarily decomposed."},{"leftTitle":"General learning and goodwill","leftText":"May be described according to actual tasks and ordinary meanings.","rightTitle":"Formal eligibility as a historical increment or as open-ended undertaking action","rightText":"Constrains only its narrow conditions, not all knowledge or the value of people."}],"boundaries":["Slowly changing behavior must not be used to presume heredity, essence, or impossibility of change; no personality diagnoses of real people are made.","Investigating situations first does not give situations permanent explanatory priority; comparisons require proportionate information and conditions.","High cost does not prove sincerity, love, or identification; low-cost help does not lose value because a narrow condition is unmet.","A real action does not automatically establish safe refusal, valid consent, or complete responsibility.","This lesson conducted no human experiment, arranged no deception, and imposed no punishment; historical research is interpreted only within the original reports’ limits."],"questions":["Why does a behavior being useful now not prove that it originated for that function?","Can actual action and free consent be respectively true and false?","In the identical-column example, what does no increment from adding P after S establish?","If experimental hurry instructions produce a difference, may it be called a pure time effect directly?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/figures/section-d-06.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual original research: a limited comparison of hurry conditions and helping behavior","href":"https://sparq.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj19021/files/media/file/darley_batson_1973_-_from_jerusalem_to_jericho.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual original research: a limited comparison of hurry conditions and helping behavior","text":"The experiment reported by Darley and Batson in 1973 was actually conducted on December 14–16, 1970. The original paper states that 47 people entered the experimental arrangements and that data for 7 were excluded because of procedural contamination or suspicion about the situation, leaving 40 for analysis. Hurry levels were manipulated through different instructions; the authors reported differences in helping responses associated with them. The result is suitable for examining situational conditions, but cannot establish the absence of all personality factors or separately identify pure time pressure.","sourceLabel":"Darley & Batson, JPSP 27(1), 100–108, scan of the original 1973 publication","sourceUrl":"https://sparq.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj19021/files/media/file/darley_batson_1973_-_from_jerusalem_to_jericho.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed p102, Subjects; p103, Scheduling and footnote 3; pp103–104, Variable 2: Hurry; pp104–105, Overall Helping Behavior","boundary":"A small sample of seminary students in a specific situation; footnote 3 discloses an assignment error that increased the moderate-hurry group. This guide does not reestimate statistics, claim universal replication, or adopt unidentified personality essences or single-mediator conclusions.","href":"https://sparq.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj19021/files/media/file/darley_batson_1973_-_from_jerusalem_to_jericho.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.06","start_line":5138,"end_line":5153,"startLine":5138,"endLine":5153}],"outputId":"section-d-06","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Separate a behavior’s current function, historical formation, transmission, intention, and welfare, seeking material for each question.","For cooperation and stable tendencies, specify observation and response channels, opportunities, practice, and selection and measurement across situations.","Separate actual action, safe refusal, valid consent, and responsibility, tracing the different control positions of designers, supervisors, and beneficiaries.","Disclose overlap and increments when comparing personality and situation; do not split condition-package effects into unidentified individual components.","Recognize the potential value of general learning and low-cost goodwill, without deriving normative authorization from evolutionary persistence, cost, or effects."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Expansion of the source’s teaching example: signing under credible punishment","text":"An imagined employee is told that failing to sign a record will lead to credible punishment, then signs personally. The signing behavior can be established; safe refusal and valid consent require separate judgment. The organizational designer who sets the rule, the supervisor who applies pressure, and the beneficiary who receives gains may have different responsibilities. A signature cannot compress the entire control chain into the employee’s sole responsibility.","assumptions":"Follows the source’s teaching example; no real employee or punishment is involved.","boundary":"No legal liability is adjudicated; actual responsibility requires applicable norms and facts."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: overlap does not mean one side is nonexistent","text":"Let four constructed records have situation code S=(0,0,1,1), tendency proxy P=(0,0,1,1), and behavior Y=(0,0,1,1). S alone fits Y perfectly, and adding P provides no further fit gain; using P first and adding S gives the same result. Thus zero increment cannot distinguish which variable has an independent real mechanism, much less establish that the other kind of factor is nonexistent.","assumptions":"The three identical sequences are a formal counterexample, not personality-measurement data or a causal model.","boundary":"No real effects of personality or situation are estimated; real identification needs independent variation, measurement, and appropriate design."},{"title":"Teaching construction: low-cost goodwill still has value","text":"An imagined coworker brings back a needed document while already going that way. There is no major self-sacrifice, but it does spare the other person one trip. It can be described as ordinary help without first proving high cost or a formal result of open-ended undertaking action. Further claims about changed long-term relationships or institutional feedback would still need corresponding material.","assumptions":"The people and assistance are teaching assumptions.","boundary":"One small act of help does not establish personality essence or repaired long-term relationships."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"This habit has remained unchanged for years, so it must be hereditary and unchangeable.","effect":"Stability alone cannot distinguish mechanisms such as practice, environmental selection, opportunity, and heredity.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"A personality proxy adds no increment after situation is entered first, so personality is entirely nonexistent.","effect":"Allocation of shared explanation depends on representation and ordering; the formal identical-column example refutes that inference.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The employee signed personally, so the institutional designer and supervisor no longer have responsibility.","effect":"The fact of action does not erase refusal conditions or responsibility across levels of control.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does a behavior being useful now not prove that it originated for that function?","answer":"Current consequences and historical formation are different questions; functions may have changed, and other formation processes may exist.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can actual action and free consent be respectively true and false?","answer":"Yes. A constrained actor may have local agency without safe refusal conditions; these need separate judgments.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"In the identical-column example, what does no increment from adding P after S establish?","answer":"In that data representation, P adds no fitting information. It does not identify whether personality or situation is nonexistent, or which is causal.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If experimental hurry instructions produce a difference, may it be called a pure time effect directly?","answer":"Not automatically. Instructions may change several conditions simultaneously; individual components or mediators require additional identification.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/figures/section-d-06.png","alt":"Separate function, formation, and transmission from intention and welfare. Personality as conditional tendency: slow change ≠ heredity or essence; situation and practice also require checks. Four judgments: actual action, safe refusal, valid consent, and responsibility. Shared explanation is not exclusive; zero increment ≠ nonexistence of a factor. Condition-package effects: do not split them into unidentified individual components. Cost does not prove love or sincerity; evolutionary selection generates no ought; general learning can still hold. Drawn by the author of this guide from D.06; the source’s employee example and identical-column sequences are teaching constructions, not diagnoses of real personalities.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from D.06; the source’s employee example and identical-column sequences are teaching constructions, not diagnoses of real personalities."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Toggling safe-refusal availability, whether data columns are identical, and whether manipulation has multiple components could show corresponding inference scope and unresolved responsibility.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Specific original research is used to constrain extrapolation concerning condition packages and personality explanations; the experiment was not replicated, and subjects’ behavior was not turned into moral labels."},"openQuestions":["The original guide did not recheck the study’s individual raw data, long-term replication, or every personality-measurement detail; only the listed methods and results passages were read.","Not all original studies corresponding to 06.01–06.08 were obtained, so the complete history of intellectual adoption cannot be reconstructed."],"taskId":"S-D-06","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 4609–4624; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["D.06: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"darley-batson1973","title":"From Jerusalem to Jericho: A Study of Situational and Dispositional Variables in Helping Behavior","url":"https://sparq.stanford.edu/sites/g/files/sbiybj19021/files/media/file/darley_batson_1973_-_from_jerusalem_to_jericho.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"JPSP 27(1), 100–108, 1973; study conducted 1970-12-14–16","locator":"pp102–105: Subjects; Scheduling and footnote 3; Variable 2: Hurry; Overall Helping Behavior","claim_support":["The original study’s sample selection, experiment dates, hurry instructions, and bounded helping outcomes"],"limits":["Raw data were not rechecked; no claim is made that all personality effects are invalid or that a separate time mechanism is established. The original paper discloses a random-assignment error; applicable population and context are limited."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"D.06　人性、演化、习惯与行动能力; Chinese source lines 5082–5097; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-06/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a93c86b16d43ad35af71f1662462ad1a60243cc6a684eac55f0011ff31284f71","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-06.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-06.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-06.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(t,{section:"D.06"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const g=n(l,[["render",d]]);export{b as __pageData,g as default};
